{"name":"Swallow Blue","hex":"#154962","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"swallow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cause (food, drink etc.) to pass from the mouth into the stomach; to take into the stomach through the throat. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","To take (something) in so that it disappears; to consume, absorb."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The amount swallowed in one gulp; the act of swallowing.","A deep chasm or abyss in the earth."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/swallow"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Swallow","blocks":[{"text":"The swallows, martins, and saw-wings, or Hirundinidae, are a family of passerine songbirds found around the world on all continents, including occasionally in Antarctica. Highly adapted to aerial feeding, they have a distinctive appearance. The term \"swallow\" is used as the common name for Hirundo rustica in the United Kingdom and Ireland. Around 90 species of Hirundinidae are known, divided into 21 genera, with the greatest diversity found in Africa, which is also thought to be where they evolved as hole-nesters. They also occur on a number of oceanic islands. A number of European and North American species are long-distance migrants; by contrast, the West and South African swallows are nonmigratory."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Pied-winged_swallow_%28Hirundo_leucosoma%29.jpg/330px-Pied-winged_swallow_%28Hirundo_leucosoma%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swallow"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Riparia_riparia.jpg/500px-Riparia_riparia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Riparia_riparia.jpg/500px-Riparia_riparia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The bill of the sand martin is typical for the family, being short and wide.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARiparia_riparia.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Hirundo_abyssinica.jpg/500px-Hirundo_abyssinica.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Hirundo_abyssinica.jpg/1280px-Hirundo_abyssinica.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Hirundo_abyssinica.jpg/500px-Hirundo_abyssinica.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The lesser striped swallow is a partial migrant within Africa.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHirundo_abyssinica.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Adult_Northern_Rough-winged_Swallow_%28Stelgidopteryx_serripennis%29_in_Waterville%2C_Maine.jpg/500px-Adult_Northern_Rough-winged_Swallow_%28Stelgidopteryx_serripennis%29_in_Waterville%2C_Maine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Adult_Northern_Rough-winged_Swallow_%28Stelgidopteryx_serripennis%29_in_Waterville%2C_Maine.jpg/1280px-Adult_Northern_Rough-winged_Swallow_%28Stelgidopteryx_serripennis%29_in_Waterville%2C_Maine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Adult_Northern_Rough-winged_Swallow_%28Stelgidopteryx_serripennis%29_in_Waterville%2C_Maine.jpg/500px-Adult_Northern_Rough-winged_Swallow_%28Stelgidopteryx_serripennis%29_in_Waterville%2C_Maine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A northern rough-winged swallow photographed in central Maine, the northeastern limit of the species' breeding range.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAdult_Northern_Rough-winged_Swallow_(Stelgidopteryx_serripennis)_in_Waterville%2C_Maine.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Tachycineta_bicolor_18319.JPG/500px-Tachycineta_bicolor_18319.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Tachycineta_bicolor_18319.JPG/500px-Tachycineta_bicolor_18319.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A tree swallow attending its nest in a tree cavity","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATachycinet